@node LibreClaw Hatch Wizard
@section Hatch Wizard

@subsection Overview

The hatch wizard scaffolds a new libreclaw workspace inside a
REPL-style org buffer.  It drives libreclaw 0.18.0's
@code{lc_hatch_*} library API through a back-and-forth conversation
with the user --- the wizard asks a question, you type your answer
below the @samp{** you} heading, press @kbd{C-c C-c}, and the next
question appears underneath.

Start it with:

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-hatch
@end example

and enter a directory path (defaults to
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-default-workspace}, i.e. @file{~/.libreclaw/}).
A buffer named @samp{*libreclaw-hatch: <dir>*} opens in
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-hatch-mode}, an @code{org-mode} derivative.

@subsection Interaction model

The buffer works exactly like a libreclaw room buffer:

@itemize
@item
History (everything above the most recent @samp{** you} heading)
is read-only --- the wizard rejects edits there with
@code{text-read-only}.
@item
The region below the current @samp{** you} heading is an editable
compose area.  @kbd{RET} is ordinary org-mode, so you can write
multi-line answers, paste source blocks, etc.
@item
@kbd{C-c C-c} submits the compose region.  The wizard validates
the answer, calls the relevant @code{lc_hatch_*} DEFUN, and
inserts the next @samp{** wizard:} prompt + a fresh @samp{** you}
compose region below.
@item
@kbd{C-c C-k} aborts the session at any time.  Killing the buffer
directly is also safe --- a @code{kill-buffer-hook} releases the
underlying @code{LcHatchContext} via
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-hatch-free}.
@end itemize

@subsection Buffer layout (mid-session)

@example
#+TITLE: LibreClaw Workspace Hatching
#+STARTUP: showall indent

* Welcome
I'll help you scaffold a new libreclaw workspace at
=/home/me/.libreclaw/my-bot=.

Answer each question below the `** you' heading and press
`C-c C-c' to submit...

* Wizard                                              :wizard:

** wizard: What should this workspace be called?
Short identifier matching "^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_-]*$".
Example: my-bot

** you
my-bot

** wizard: Attach an identity file (SOUL.md)?
Type a path to a SOUL.md file, or "skip" to continue without one.

** you
skip

** wizard: Which AI provider should this workspace use?
Type one of: claude, openai, both

** you
<cursor parks here; user types the answer>
@end example

@subsection Conversation flow

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Workspace name} --- validated against
@code{^[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_-]*$}.  Retries on invalid input.
@item
@strong{Identity (SOUL.md)} --- path or @samp{skip}.
@item
@strong{AI provider} --- @samp{claude}, @samp{openai}, or
@samp{both}.
@item
@strong{Channels} --- loops until you type @samp{done}:
@itemize
@item
@samp{cmacs} --- the native in-process Emacs channel.  No
parameters.  Recommended for in-Emacs workflows; the generated
YAML is simply @code{channels: cmacs: enabled: true} and the
Emacs host handles rooms, persistence, and buffer rendering
entirely at runtime.
@item
@samp{matrix} --- asks for homeserver, then user ID.  Token is
prompted via @code{read-passwd} in the @emph{minibuffer} and
stored via @code{auth-source}.  YAML only references
@code{$@{auth-source:matrix:@@user:host@}}, never the raw token.
@item
@samp{local} --- asks for the prompt string.  CLI-only: uses
fd 0/1, so it's not useful inside Emacs.  Use @samp{cmacs}
instead.
@item
@samp{email} --- asks for IMAP host, SMTP host, username.
Password via @code{read-passwd} + @code{auth-source}.
@item
@samp{webhook} --- asks for port and path prefix.
@end itemize
@item
@strong{Podomation} --- yes/no; on yes, asks for an inline DSL
block (or @samp{none} to enable with no pre-declared pods).
@item
@strong{Audit log} --- yes/no; on yes, asks for the SQLite path
(or @samp{default}).
@item
@strong{Review & Write} --- wizard inserts the proposed YAML
inline as a @code{#+begin_src yaml} block and asks yes/no to
finalize.  On yes, @code{lc_hatch_finalize} writes the workspace
and offers to @code{cmacs-libreclaw-start} against the new config.
@end enumerate

Any step that rejects the answer re-asks the same question inline
with the error message.  No lost context, no modal dialogs.

@subsection Keybindings

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-hatch-submit}
@item C-c C-k
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-hatch-cancel}
@item RET
org-mode default --- newline, for multi-line answers
@item TAB
org-mode default --- visibility cycling / source-block indent
@end table

There are no step-navigation bindings --- the wizard is a linear
conversational flow, and history is read-only.  To change an
earlier answer, cancel with @kbd{C-c C-k} and restart.

@subsection Secrets handling

@itemize
@item
Matrix access tokens and email passwords are read via
@code{read-passwd} (no echo) in the minibuffer, never the org
buffer.
@item
The hatch mode disables @code{auto-save-mode} and
@code{make-backup-files} locally, so even transient buffer state
doesn't hit disk.
@item
After reading, the wizard tries
@code{auth-source-secrets-create}/@code{save-function} to store
the value in your configured backend.
@item
The YAML written to disk contains only the indirection
@code{$@{auth-source:matrix:@@user:host@}} --- libreclaw fetches
the real value at load time.
@end itemize

@subsection Non-wizard path

If you just want a minimal working YAML with a Local channel and
no credentials, call @kbd{M-x cmacs-libreclaw-generate-default-config}
instead.  The wizard is for the richer case where you're
configuring Matrix tokens, email credentials, podomation rules,
and so on.
